   "label": "KV pool in virtual memory",
   "doc": "Places the KV pool's cache planes in a CUDA virtual-memory reservation instead of ordinary device allocations: each plane has a fixed stretch of address space sized for the largest pool the Spark could hold, and only the front of it is backed by memory. Same pool size, same bytes, same layout of planes; the planes' addresses never change, which is what lets a later live resize keep every captured graph. Costs up to one allocation granule (2 MiB) of rounding per plane. On when set to anything but 0, on every rank (a follower: the environment).",
